   299	**3** **APPROACH**
   300	
   301	The goal of our approach is to find a path of UI events in an app that will reproduce the steps
   302	leading to the failure described in a bug report. As discussed in Section 2, existing approaches are
   303	limited by their sub-optimal search strategies that may not find the best matching sequence when
   304	bug reports have inaccurate or missing steps. Our first insight is that the quality of the sequence
   305	matching can be improved by searching globally through the UI Transition Graph ( UTG ) of the
   306	app, a model of all of the UI s and their transitions (i.e., UI events) [ 18, 33, 49 ], for the best possible
   307	match of the bug report steps. This can avoid the limitations of existing approaches’ dynamic search
   308	techniques, which can get trapped in local maxima best matches. However, such a global search
   309	is computationally expensive since the scale of modern apps and their UI s can be very large. Our
   310	second insight is that the problem of finding the best matching sequence of events can be solved
   311	efficiently by (1) modeling the matches between UI events and bug report steps as a Markov model,
   312	with the quality of the match represented by the model’s transition probability function, and then
   313	(2) using dynamic programming techniques to solve for the best paths in the model, which would
   314	correspond to the best-matching UI event sequences. However, such an approach can only match
   315	UI events to steps that are not missing from the bug report. To address the problem of missing
   316	steps, our third insight is that the UTG can again be employed to find sub-paths of UI events that
   317	connect pairs of UI events that have been matched to bug report steps.
